analysis:
Only test files changed in this batch. scripts/test-provider-release-resolution.py head 107-121 adds expectations that a workflow_dispatch on refs/heads/dev resolves as a published dev-branch build (0.9.0-dev.branch.N, publish=true), ignores RELEASE_VALIDATION_ONLY and RELEASE_VERSION_OVERRIDE, and fails closed for empty, prod or staging environments. scripts/test-provider-release-resume.py (patch hunk @@ -158,11 +161,22 @@) expects verify_workflow_source to admit a dev dispatch only for RELEASE_ENVIRONMENT=dev and only when GITHUB_SHA equals the current dev HEAD ('current dev commit'). Base line 165 previously asserted that a dev dispatch was refused ('current reviewed master'); the head now uses schedule in that case instead. FakeAPI adds commits/fff and an identical compare fixture.

Security-relevant change: this widens who can publish a signed dev release. Before, publication required a push to dev. Now anyone with workflow_dispatch rights can trigger it on the dev branch, at its current HEAD. The tests still keep a dispatch on dev away from staging/prod signing (A-011/A-017 release identity, T-054). They also still require a GitHub-verified commit, through the shared self.commit fixture.

The actual implementations were not supplied: resolve-provider-release.sh, provider_release_resume/api.py and release workflow YAML. I therefore cannot confirm the following:
- that dev dispatch still requires commit verification;
- that RELEASE_ENVIRONMENT cannot be set by the dispatcher to reach prod secrets;
- that the workflow's environment protections and secret scoping stop a dev-branch dispatch from using the staging/prod signing identity.

The tests show fail-closed intent. There is no visible regression in the test code itself. Integration should examine the changed resolver/api/workflow sources for:
- the dev-dispatch branch;
- the HEAD-equality check against git/ref/heads/dev;
- whether the dev signing identity or dev releases are trusted by any coordinator lane outside DevNet.
